<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samosata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsat is a town in Adıyaman province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. It was the capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Kummuh and later of the Hellenistic kingdom of Commagene, and the birthplace of the satirist Lucian. The ancient city mound was flooded by the reservoir of the Atatürk Dam in the late 1980s, and the town was rebuilt nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsat.jpg" alt="Samsat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radosław Botev · CC BY 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsat_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> was founded some time before 245 BCE on the earlier Neo-Hittite site of Kummuh by Sames I, the Orontid king of Sophene, perhaps to assert his claim over the area. It became one of the Orontid kings' most important royal residences, built in a Persian architectural style, and its architecture shifted when the Orontids of Commagene drew closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a>. Antiochus I Theos, who reigned from 70 to 31 BCE, founded sanctuaries here with inscriptions about his cult and reliefs of his handshake with Apollo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a>. In 73 CE Samosata and the rest of Commagene were incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and under Hadrian it was given the status of metropolis, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>.</p><p>In 260 it was the first city sacked by the Sasanian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> after he captured the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>. Julian II had ships built here for his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>. Lucian (c. 120–192) and Paul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a>, active around 260, were born here. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took Commagene from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 640, and in June 966 the town hosted an exchange of prisoners between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>.</p><p>In the early 12th century it was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> and joined the county of Edessa. Warfare devastated it in the 13th century: the Anatolian Seljuks took it in 1203, the Anushtegins looted it in 1237 and Hülagü Khan conquered it in 1240. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> briefly absorbed it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1392, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> destroyed it in 1401, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> recaptured it in 1516 and renamed it Samsat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town was evacuated on 5 March 1988 and the old settlement was submerged in 1989 by the Atatürk Reservoir; a new town was built beside the new waterline. An earthquake destroyed it on 2 March 2017, and it was largely rebuilt. Its population was 3,790 in 2022. The tell, Samsat Höyük, was excavated from 1978 to 1987 under Ankara University and yielded evidence of settlement from the Halaf period to the Ottoman period; it now lies under the reservoir, and the old town below it was never excavated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samsat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hahhum lay on the upper Euphrates, probably near <b>Samsat</b> in south-eastern Turkey, where the caravan routes from Assur crossed the river into Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/hahhum">Hahhum</a></li><li><blockquote>Kummuh arose after the Hittite collapse c. 1180 BCE in the land on the right bank of the upper Euphrates around Samosata, modern <b>Samsat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kummuh">Kummuh</a></li></ul></section>
